---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ScoreConverter from '../components/ScoreConverter.svelte';
---

<BaseLayout title="Score Converter" description="Convert Deluge song arrangements to MusicXML sheet music — entirely in your browser.">
  <section class="page-header">
    <h1 class="page-title">Score Converter</h1>
    <p class="page-desc">Convert Deluge songs to MusicXML. Open in MuseScore or other notation app.</p>
    <details class="page-details">
        <summary>How it works &amp; limitations</summary>
        <ul>
          <li>Like <a href="/preview">Song Preview</a>, this an experimental feature originally intended to make it easier to know which song is which on the Deluge SD card.</li>
          <li>I've tested with MuseScore. It's been a long time since I had access to paid notation software. It presumably works with Finale, Sibelius, et al, but I don't have access to them for testing.</li>
          <li>It works best on songs with arrangements; a best-effort attempt is made to convert clip-only tracks.</li>
          <li>All tracks will default to a piano sound except percussions, which will use a basic click sound.</li>
          <li>You will see some crazy amounts of ledger lines and other weird notation choices. If you're looking for an "Official Score" for your piece, this will get you started, but expect to have cleanup work to do.</li>
        </ul>
      </details>
  </section>
  <section class="tool-area">
    <ScoreConverter client:only="svelte" />
  </section>
  <section class="info">
    <div class="info-inner">
      <div class="info-block">
        <h3>How it works</h3>
        <p>Drop a <code>.XML</code> song file from your Deluge SD card. The tool parses the arrangement view — clip instances, note data, instruments — and generates MusicXML with proper time signatures, key signatures, and tuplet notation.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="info-block">
        <h3>What you need</h3>
        <p>A song file saved in <strong>arrangement view</strong>. Songs saved only in clip/session view don't have a timeline to convert. The file is the <code>.XML</code> in your <code>SONGS</code> folder on the SD card.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="info-block">
        <h3>Privacy</h3>
        <p>Your file is processed entirely in-browser using Python compiled to WebAssembly. Nothing is uploaded anywhere. First load downloads the runtime (~15MB, cached after that).</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-header {
    max-width: 600px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 3rem 1.5rem 2rem;
    text-align: center;
  }
  .page-title {
    font-family: 'DM Mono', monospace;
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
  .page-desc {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .tool-area {
    max-width: 600px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 1.5rem 3rem;
  }

  .info {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding: 3rem 1.5rem;
  }
  .info-inner {
    max-width: 960px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(250px, 1fr));
    gap: 2rem;
  }
  .info-block h3 {
    font-family: 'DM Mono', monospace;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }
  .info-block p {
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.55;
  }
</style>
